    content: 'When transitioning away from traditional wooden decks, homeowners are immediately hit with an overwhelming array of highly marketed composite brands. Trex, TimberTech, and AZEK dominate the market. While they all offer splinter-free, rot-resistant solutions, the engineering under the hood is fundamentally different.\n\n## Trex: The Pioneer of Composite\n\nTrex invented the composite market and remains the most recognizable name. Standard Trex boards are a blend of 95% recycled plastic film and reclaimed sawdust, encased in a hard plastic capping. \n**Pros:** Highly eco-friendly, enormous color selection, and the Trex Enhance line is incredibly budget-friendly.\n**Cons:** Because it contains organic wood flour, older, uncapped generations (or boards cut at the ends) can still occasionally absorb moisture if installed incorrectly.\n\n## TimberTech: The Advanced Formulation\n\nTimberTech (now part of the AZEK company) offers boards that are capped on all four sides (unlike Trex, which usually caps three). This full encapsulation makes them extraordinarily resistant to moisture.\n**Pros:** Unprecedented mold resistance and some of the most hyper-realistic wire-brushed wood grain textures in the industry (especially the Vintage Collection).\n**Cons:** Generally carries a steeper price tag than baseline Trex models.\n\n## AZEK: The PVC Powerhouse\n\nAZEK boards are not composite at all; they are 100% cellular PVC. They contain absolutely zero organic wood fibers.\n**Pros:** They are immune to rot, mold, and insect damage. The material dissipates heat rapidly, making it significantly cooler on bare feet under the Virginia sun than dense composites.\n**Cons:** It is the highest-tier luxury product and commands a premium price point to match.\n\nFor the vast majority of our Loudoun County projects, TimberTech offers the absolute best balance of extreme luxury aesthetics and long-term durability.'
